   200	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   201	    """
   202	    Slide each monochrome sprite upward in area order, then let larger settled bboxes hide smaller ones in their overlap zones.
   203	
   204	    Input structure:
   205	    - A black background contains a full maroon bar across row 0.
   206	    - Below the bar sit 4-connected monochrome sprites of distinct colors, with distinct areas.
   207	    - Each sprite is treated by its own pixel shape and bounding box, using ordinary (row, col) coordinates.
   208	
   209	    Rule:
   210	    - Count each sprite's pixels and sort sprites from largest area to smallest; ties break by the input bbox's top row, then left column.
   211	    - Process sprites in that order, sliding each one straight upward until its bbox top reaches row 1 or one more upward step would place one of its pixels onto a cell already occupied by an earlier settled sprite.
   212	    - After all sprites settle, keep the larger sprite visible inside every final bounding-box overlap rectangle and erase the smaller sprite's pixels from that overlapped screen region.
   213	    - Keep the maroon top bar unchanged and clear all source positions.
   214	
   215	    Key insight:
   216	    - The same visible property, sprite area, governs the whole pipeline: it decides move order, which settled shapes become later obstacles, and which sprite wins any final overlap zone.
